  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nIR shows improved skies, mainly east of the Divide with more \nample cloud cover to push in from the west once again later \nSunday morning and into the afternoon hours, overnight and into\nMonday. WV shows the main PFJ still pushing around the main low\noff the Pacific northwest coast and extending meridionally \nacross the northern Rockies through Wyoming. Relatively weak in\nspeeds, and thus, a couple subtle shortwaves for later Sunday \nand again Wednesday. Ample cloud coverage increasing once again \nfrom the west, spreading east through the day with any limited \nsnow chances constrained to Yellowstone to northern Absarokas \n(10-20% coverage). The next shortwave for Monday looks to dip a \nbit further south, allowing for wider range of snow chances for \nwestern mountains up to over 30% coverage but limited in \naccumulations at this time. Highest of the elevations, to \ninclude the Tetons and western Winds looks to be an inch or two \nat best as this last piece of energy exits east with its more \nprogressive and open nature as it extends into the High Plains \nand Dakotas.\n\nInduced upper level ridging builds in late Monday into Tuesday\nwith increasing convergence aloft pushing through Wednesday as\nwell. A slight warming trend (~5 to 10 degrees warmer than\ncurrently) and dry conditions expected during this time period.\nRelatively light winds as well and more ample sunshine to be had\nfor any travel to occur for the Thanksgiving holiday and\nwatching of the Dallas Cowboys. \n\nThe next system is trending slower with the recent model runs,\nwith more of a northwest flow for late Thursday and into Friday.\nAny snow chances look to be limited to northeastern portions of\nthe CWA and beyond until the main low pushes inland from the\nPacific northwest coast. OVernight Friday into Saturday morning\nlooks to be more of the start of any more significant snowfall\nbut limited to west of the Divide with the zonal westerly flow\nwith this system. The PFJ becomes more disjointed there after\nand sags south through Wyoming with more of a weak meridional\nflow later next weekend, so will be interesting to watch model\ntrends going forward for points east of the Divide. Until then,\nstay tuned for updates and the not so impactful weather heading\ntowards Turkey Day.",
